speechtext:
Before we continue our proceedings, I have an announcement which I feel may be of some interest to hon. members, having to do with a decision to bring the East Block under the jurisdiction of parliament.
I think it is safe to say that a great majority of members on all sides of this House and in the Senate have found it anomalous that this building, which is so evidently an integral part of Parliament Hill, remains outside our jurisdiction, while we continue to occupy buildings outside this
Oral Questions
area as necessary accommodation for members and support staff.
It was, therefore, not surprising that this opinion came through quite clearly during the deliberations of the Abbott Commission on Parliamentary Accommodation which is headed by the very distinguished former minister of this House-in which capacity he incidentally occupied an office in the East Block-and consists of Speakers of both Houses of Parliament, as well as many members, senators and former parliamentarians of all parties of this House. It also came to light during discussions in the Abbott commission that a number of conditions were coming together in this year which might maximize the opportunity to bring this building within parliamentary jurisdiction. After consultation with the Speaker of the Senate and with House leaders and other members, I raised the question with the Prime Minister in a letter dated March 18, 1976, a copy of which I am tabling for the information of hon. members.
The Prime Minister acknowledged my letter on March 26 on an interim basis, and followed that with a letter to me dated May 15, indicating that the request had been favourably received, subject to certain reservations, the most significant of which are as follows:
(1)	the future occupancy of the East Block should be predominantly by parliamentarians and necessary support staff, rather than by support services;
(2)	the historic quality of the East Block should be preserved in accordance with the existing plan developed by the Department of Public Works;
(3)	more particularly, the rooms in the East Block that have been identified as having historic significance should be restored and open to the public;
(4)	amongst these rooms, the East Block cabinet chamber, the ante-chamber, the Clerk's office and adjoining office, should not only be preserved for their historic interest but should be available as required for occasional use by cabinet committees and particularly for ceremonial occasions;
(5)	improved cabinet and cabinet committee facilities should be provided for in the Centre Block.
I should also tell hon. members that, as members of the Senate are obviously involved, the Speaker of the Senate will be advising members of that House of these developments, through a similar announcement at this next sitting.
I trust that all hon. members will welcome this event which will bring this very historic building into the parliamentary ambit.
